    BASH COMMANDS

virtual enviroment: source .venv/bin/activate
deactivate

launch terminal: super + t
new tab: shift + ctrl + t
zoom in:
zoom out: ctrl + +

left tab: ctrl + shift + page up
right tab: ctrl + shift + page down
previous: ctrl + page up
next: ctrl + page down

cat - print contents of file
sort 
uniq

help - internally defined bash commands 
man [command] - command manual
which - locate a command (FOR PROGRAMS/ EXECTUTABLES. searches the folders in order in $PATH )
find - finds files
lspci - list all PCI devices (hardware connected via PCI bus like GPU, network card, etc.) (Peripheral Component Interconnect)
rfkill list - check whether wireless devices (like Wi-Fi or Bluetooth) are blocked or enabled.
grep - searches in file. Takes in regular expression
grep -i -r
ip a - protocol (IP or IPv6) address on a device.
sudo dmesg - print or control the kernel ring buffer
exiftool -b -JpgFromRaw -w jpg -ext CR3 .  // creates jpg files from cannon raw files
rm -r - delete a folder and everything inside it
sed - stream editor
tail - print end of file
head -print start of file
chmod 644 [file]   (r=4, w=2, x=1)
chmod +x
chown - change ownership
sed- stream editor used to perform basic text transformations on an input stream. sed=line editor (change names of files)


    System monitoring
& - run in background
ps - process status
ps -a for all   // proesses attached to terminal!
ps -p [pid] -f // check
top - display linux tasks
df- disk space
du - directory usage
free - free space
lsof - (list open files) powerful Linux/Unix utility used to identify which processes are using specific files, directories, or network ports. Because "everything is a file" in Unix-like systems, lsof can also track network sockets, devices, and pipes
lsof -i -P -n | grep LISTEN // see which ports the server is listening on
lsof -t -i:3000 // returns pid of service on port 3000
ps -p 22977 -f

    Networking
ping -send echo request to host
curl - used to transfer data from or to a server using various protocols like HTTP, HTTPS, FTP, and more.
wget - download files from the web
ssh
scp - transer files between systems


